2. Read the complete Java counterpart.
3. Trace methods called by both implementations when necessary.
4. Compare the actual execution logic.

Check every:

* if condition
* else branch
* switch case
* loop
* early return
* validation
* null check
* empty check
* default value
* calculation
* conversion
* mapping
* filtering
* sorting
* collection handling
* database operation
* transaction
* external call
* retry
* timeout
* exception
* catch block
* fallback
* state change
* configuration-dependent behavior

Do not assume two pieces of code are equivalent because they look similar.

For example, if .NET has:

condition A
-> operation X
condition B
-> operation Y
otherwise
-> operation Z

and Java only implements X and Y, report Z as missing.

Also check for behavior hidden in:

* helper methods
* utility classes
* extension methods
* base classes
* interfaces
* overridden methods
* callbacks
* delegates
* event handlers
* middleware
* filters
* attributes
* dependency injection registrations

Trace the actual call chain when required.

